# Kani/trust-mc local artifact outputs
*.rlib
*.symtab.smt2
# ay writes an Alethe certificate next to the input on UNSAT. Nothing consumes
# it — the driver reads sat/unsat and no more — but it is no longer suppressed:
# `--no-proof` stopped disabling ay's proof tracker and had become a net cost,
# so the flag was dropped. These land in tests/expected/ during a run.
*.alethe
# ...and the partial files ay writes while producing one.
*.alethe.tmp-*
*.symtab.z3_fixed.smt2
# The one that got away: a corpus run left a `*.symtab.cover_check.smt2` beside
# a test and `git add -A` committed it (97ec8a7a5, removed in the commit that
# added this line). The two rules above already covered its siblings.
#
# Deliberately NOT ignoring `*.symtab.vc.json` or `*.symtab.out`, which look
# like the same family: tools/soundness-duals/ commits .symtab.vc.json files on
# purpose, and a blanket rule would silently swallow the next one added.
*.symtab.cover_check.smt2
# Same family, same story: running a dual harness leaves a
# `<crate>.kani-metadata.json` beside its source and `git add -A` committed one
# (tools/soundness-duals/dual_d_field_ref_body.kani-metadata.json, removed in the
# commit that added this line). It is pure build output — nothing in the tree
# reads it — and it embeds ABSOLUTE `original_file` / `model_file` paths from
# the machine that generated it, which is how it failed the publication
# forbidden-content guard.
*.kani-metadata.json
mir_dump/
out/
doc/

# Compiletest output (per-harness JSON/TSV reports)
reports/

# Crash dumps and temp artifacts
rustc-ice-*.txt
*.mir
pytest-of-*/
result_output_dir/

# Agent worktrees + local Claude state (never commit)
.claude/worktrees/

# Publish tool work area (export tree, reports, staging clone)
publish/.out/

# ay-chc proof certificates + their lock files, regenerated per verification run
*.chccert
*.chccert.ay-chc.lock
